Iraqi Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi is considering sending army units to sites in the Kurdistan Region of Iraq that the government says are being used to smuggle oil, Iraqi sources familiar with the matter told MBN. According to political sources close to al-Zaidi, "the prime minister sought in recent days to move army units toward&hellip;
Al-Zaidi sent Popular Mobilization Forces Commission chief Faleh al-Fayyad to the region carrying a message urging local authorities to take control of the smuggling issue and cooperate with the federal government, the sources said. It was not immediately clear what response, if any, Kurdish authorities had given.
The move is part of Baghdad’s efforts, announced on Tuesday, to transfer control of the Kurdish region’s oil exports, border crossings and oil fields to federal authorities.
